      ":question/worked-solution": "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $9$ as ${3}^{2},$ and then rewrite the left-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{x-5} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ {({3}^{2})}^{x-5} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ {3}^{2(x-5)} & ={3}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-5) & =x \\\\ 2x-10 & =x \\\\ x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$",
